@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s
@title LaTeX # & _ Demonstration

@s Distance & escaping

This **small [guide](https://example.com/a_b?x=1&y=2)
matters**, as does *multiline
emphasis*. Literal code preserves `  @{not active}  `, while
@code{value_1 & value_2} is Rust. The equation $d^2 = x^2 + y^2$ remains TeX.
The implementation is in @{Compute_value #1}. An [unsafe](javascript:alert)
target stays visible. Unicode prose includes café and an em dash — safely.

- A list item uses @code{Graph}.
- A second item continues
  on another physical line.

3. Ordered values may begin elsewhere.
4. Their numbering continues.

```text
literal \command { braces } % # $ & _ ^ ~
This deliberately long fixed-width example line contains enough words and symbols to exceed an ordinary printed measure without disappearing beyond the right edge.
```

| Form | Meaning | Count |
| :--- | :---: | ---: |
| `a_b` | *centered* | 2 |
| @code{Graph} | [safe](guide.html) | $n+1$ |

$$
\begin{aligned}
d^2 &= x^2 + y^2 \\
    &= 25
\end{aligned}
$$

--- demo.rs
fn main() {
    @{Compute_value #1}
    let path = r"C:\very\long\path\whose\contents\make\this\source\line\wrap\without\becoming\active\LaTeX";
    println!("{path}");
}
---

--- Compute_value #1
struct Graph {
    value_1: i32,
}

fn squared_distance(x: i32, y: i32) -> i32 {
    x * x + y * y
}
---

--- Compute_value #1 +=
fn graph_value(graph: &Graph) -> i32 {
    graph.value_1
}
